label: Resolve Identity
description: >-
  ##### THE FAN-IN #####

  Join the normalized parcel with its resolved township into a complete
  identity block, and combine their provenances -- the output is only as
  trustworthy as the weaker input (combineProvenance,
  packages/schema/src/provenance.ts).

  This is blue-ribbon's provenance lattice meet expressed as weir's readiness
  condition, and the two line up well. `combineProvenance` is literally a fold
  taking the minimum rank over its inputs; `input: allOf` is literally "fire
  when all of them exist". The algebra and the wiring agree.

  ##### BUT: A LINEAGE QUESTION I COULD NOT ANSWER FROM THE DOCS #####

  This is a DIAMOND WITH ONE SHORT SIDE:

      NormalizedParcel ---------------------------+
             |                                    |
             +--> ParcelCentroid --> TownshipLookup --> resolveIdentity

  One of the two inputs is the ANCESTOR of the other. The readme says a fan-in
  fires "once per lineage group -- instances grouped by nearest common ancestor
  and zipped positionally". The nearest common ancestor of NormalizedParcel#1
  and TownshipLookup#1 is NormalizedParcel#1 -- itself. Does an instance form a
  lineage group with its own descendant, or does grouping assume the two inputs
  are siblings the way Dough and Oven are in the recipe?

  I could not tell from readme.md or the join spec, so I RAN IT.

  ##### ANSWER: IT WORKS. #####

  Run brp-1, 6 firings / 6 pulses, no residue. The log:

    ProxiedCountyQuery  step 1  <- [Run]
    RawParcelFeature    step 2  <- [ProxiedCountyQuery]
    NormalizedParcel    step 3  <- [RawParcelFeature]
    ParcelCentroid      step 4  <- [NormalizedParcel]
    TownshipLookup      step 5  <- [ParcelCentroid]
    ParcelIdentity      step 6  <- [NormalizedParcel#3, TownshipLookup#5]

  The fan-in cites an instance from step 3 and one from step 5 and fires
  correctly. An instance DOES form a lineage group with its own descendant.
  Leaving the question written down because it was a real doubt and the
  behaviour is not stated anywhere in readme.md or the joins spec -- every
  example in the repo joins siblings, and "carry the original forward
  alongside something derived from it" is most of what ETL is. It deserves a
  sentence in the spec and a test, since nothing currently pins it.
input:
  allOf:
    - NormalizedParcel
    - TownshipLookup
output: ParcelIdentity
examples:
  - given:
      NormalizedParcel:
        pin: "062-026-300-020-00"
        county: "Iosco"
        townshipFromSource: null
        acres: 8.51
        boundaryJson: '{"type":"Polygon","coordinates":[[[-83.4,44.4],[-83.4,44.5],[-83.3,44.5],[-83.4,44.4]]]}'
        provenance: "aggregator"
        vintageAsOf: "2026-08-30"
        vintageSourceType: "continuous"
        vintageNote: "Iosco county FeatureServer via app.fetchgis.com proxy"
      TownshipLookup:
        pin: "062-026-300-020-00"
        township: "Oscoda"
        provenance: "verified"
        vintageAsOf: "2026-08-30"
        vintageSourceType: "periodic"
        vintageNote: "statewide MCD point-in-polygon; county layer has no township field"
    expect:
      ParcelIdentity:
        pin: "062-026-300-020-00"
        county: "Iosco"
        township: "Oscoda"
        acres: 8.51
        boundaryJson: '{"type":"Polygon","coordinates":[[[-83.4,44.4],[-83.4,44.5],[-83.3,44.5],[-83.4,44.4]]]}'
        # Iosco came through the fetchgis proxy, so the parcel side is
        # `aggregator`; the township side is `verified`; the meet is
        # `aggregator`. This is the whole point of the fan-in.
        provenance: "aggregator"
        vintageAsOf: "2026-08-30"
        vintageSourceType: "continuous"
        vintageNote: "parcel: Iosco county FeatureServer via app.fetchgis.com proxy; township: statewide MCD point-in-polygon; county layer has no township field"
properties:
  - name: both inputs describe the same parcel
    description: >-
      Stated against BOTH inputs, following assembleEvidence.node's reasoning
      in soc-triage: comparing the output to only one of them is a false green,
      because an implementation handed parcel A and township B could project A
      and pass. That is exactly the cross-parcel pairing this must prevent, and
      in a corridor run with 3,265 concurrent parcels it is a real hazard
      rather than a hypothetical one.
    expr:
      and:
        - eq:
            - get: output.pin
            - get: input.NormalizedParcel.pin
        - eq:
            - get: output.pin
            - get: input.TownshipLookup.pin
  - name: combined provenance is never stronger than either input
    description: >-
      The lattice meet, asserted. This is the property blue-ribbon's
      combineProvenance has a unit test for; here it is a contract obligation
      on every implementation instead.

      FRICTION (#12): I cannot actually write this, because expressing "rank of
      X <= rank of Y" needs an ordering over an enum and the property language
      has eq/gt/and/not over paths and literals. What I want is either an
      `ordinal` field kind whose enumValues are ordered, or the ability to call
      a declared pure function from a property. Written out as prose so you can
      see the shape of what is missing.
    expr:
      eq:
        - get: output.provenance
        - get: output.provenance
